> Explain in two sentences why a GPU is faster than a CPU for training neural networks.
A GPU is faster than a CPU for training neural networks because it has many more parallel processing cores that can execute multiple operations simultaneously, allowing it to process large amounts of data and perform complex calculations more quickly.

> Write a tweet complaining about a delayed flight, then rewrite it to sound polite.
Tweet:
"Ugh, my flight from LA to NYC is still delayed by 4 hours! Can't believe this is happening. Where's my bag? I'm sooooo stressed out! 😢"

Rewritten to sound polite:
"Sorry for any inconvenience, but my flight from LA to NYC is still delayed by 4 hours. I hope to make it by the time we land in NYC. In the meantime, I'll make the most of my free time and catch up on some reading. Thanks for your understanding!"

> What is the difference between precision and recall? Give a spam-filter example.
Precision and recall are two important metrics used in evaluating the performance of a binary classifier, such as a spam filter. They are often used in conjunction with each other to provide a more complete picture of a classifier's performance.
Precision is the ratio of true positive predictions to the total number of positive predictions. In other words, it measures the proportion of positive predictions that are actually correct. A high precision means that the classifier is correctly identifying a large number of actual positive cases.
Recall, on the other hand, is the ratio of true positive predictions to the total number of actual positive cases. It measures the proportion of actual positive cases that are correctly identified by the classifier. A high recall means that the classifier is correctly identifying a large number of actual positive cases.
